---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, DASH_URL, DEFAULT_QUOTA, GITHUB_URL, SIGNUP_CURL, SITE_TITLE, X_HANDLE, X_URL } from "../consts";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`About — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="What nano-api is, who wrote it, why it is free, and the limits that exist anyway."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<div class="page">
				<p class="eyebrow">About</p>
				<h1>What this is, and why it is free</h1>

				<p>
					Every project I have worked on needed some of the same small pieces. Something to notice
					when a nightly job stops running. Something to call a URL on a schedule, in the right
					timezone. A lock, so two instances do not both do the work. One value I can change
					without a deploy. A counter. None of them is hard. All of them are fiddly, and I have
					written each one more than once, in a hurry, slightly wrong.
				</p>
				<p>
					So I wrote them again with tests, and put them behind an HTTP endpoint on infrastructure
					that does not need me awake. Take a key and use them.
				</p>
				<pre class="signup"><code>{SIGNUP_CURL}</code></pre>

				<h2>Why it is free</h2>
				<p>
					Charging is a promise. It commits me to a response time and an uptime I cannot hold down
					alongside a day job, and I would rather give you something useful with stated limits than
					something priced with vague ones. The infrastructure costs about five dollars a month
					whether anyone uses it or not.
				</p>
				<p>
					If that changes: an email well before anything costs money, nothing switched off without
					you agreeing first, and no change to the shape of the API. Billing is not a reason to
					break a client.
				</p>

				<h2>How it is built</h2>
				<p>
					One Cloudflare Worker, one D1 database, one cron trigger firing every minute for the
					sweep. No VM, no container, no queue. That is the reason a free tier is possible at all:
					there is nothing running when nobody is calling.
				</p>
				<p>
					Keys are stored as SHA-256 hashes, never in clear text, and you rotate and revoke them
					yourself. A key is full access or read-only, fixed at creation, and the read ones cannot
					issue wider ones. Where correctness depends on two callers not colliding, it is a single SQL
					statement with a <code>WHERE</code> that only one of them can satisfy, verified with
					twenty parallel requests in a test rather than asserted on a page. The code is
					<a href={GITHUB_URL} rel="noopener" target="_blank">on GitHub</a>, including the parts I
					got wrong first.
				</p>

				<h2>Limits</h2>
				<p>Technical, not commercial. They stop one account taking the service down for everyone.</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						{DEFAULT_QUOTA} monitors, schedules, configs or counters to start. Ask and it goes up;
						it is one integer on your user row and it costs nothing.
					</li>
					<li>
						The quota gates <em>creating</em> things. Pings and scheduled runs for things that
						already exist are never refused for quota reasons, because a billing state should not
						be able to switch off your monitoring quietly.
					</li>
					<li>Ping payloads 2 KB. Scheduled request bodies 8 KB.</li>
					<li>Scheduled calls: 60s timeout, 3 attempts, public HTTPS targets only.</li>
					<li>100 distinct lock names, lease up to an hour.</li>
					<li>Three signups per address per hour.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>Before you rely on it</h2>

				<div class="qa">
					<h3>How fast will I know something broke?</h3>
					<p>
						The sweep runs every minute, so an alert lands within about a minute of the deadline.
						In practice I measured 9 seconds past due on a 120-second window. If you need
						second-level precision, this is the wrong tool and I would say so.
					</p>

					<h3>Where do alerts go?</h3>
					<p>
						Any HTTPS webhook. The body has <code>text</code> at the top level, which is what a
						Slack incoming webhook expects, so Slack needs no mapping. Email and SMS are not
						built.
					</p>

					<h3>Is there a dashboard?</h3>
					<p>
						One, read-only, at <a href={DASH_URL}>dash.nano-api.com</a>. It has no login: you paste
						a key, because the key is already the account. Sessions, CSRF and a password reset flow
						are three things I would build badly, so there are none.
					</p>
					<p>
						It insists on a key with <code>scope: read</code> and refuses one that can write. That
						is the point of it. Everything else is still the API and
						<a href="/howto/">the curl walkthrough</a>.
					</p>

					<h3>What if the service itself goes down?</h3>
					<p>
						Then you are not monitored, and there is no SLA to point at. Where a missed alert
						would genuinely hurt, run two providers. I would tell a friend the same thing.
					</p>

					<h3>Do you look at my data?</h3>
					<p>
						No. Delete a monitor and it is gone, including its ping log and events. There is no
						analytics script on this site.
					</p>
				</div>

				<h2>Who</h2>
				<p>
					One person, evenings and weekends, in Norway. Bugs, questions and things I have got
					wrong go to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> or
					<a href={X_URL} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{X_HANDLE}</a>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.page {
		max-width: 46em;
		padding: 2em 0 0;
	}
	.page h2 {
		margin-top: 2.2em;
		font-size: 1.45rem;
	}
	.page p,
	.page li {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.signup {
		font-size: 0.78rem;
		margin: 1.4em 0 0;
	}
	.page ul {
		padding-left: 1.2em;
	}
	.page li {
		margin-bottom: 0.5em;
	}
	.qa h3 {
		margin-top: 1.8em;
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		color: var(--text);
	}
</style>
